__d(function(e,t,s,i,n,o,a){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"A Pact with the Sun",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"The Wonder Called Sleep",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What fraction of our lives do we spend sleeping?",options:["A) One-quarter","B) One-third","C) One-half","D) One-fifth"],correct_answer:"B) One-third",explanation:"We spend approximately one-third of our lives sleeping. For a 75-year lifespan, this is about 25 years. This fact from the chapter establishes that sleep is far too significant to be dismissed as laziness or wasted time."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does NREM stand for?",options:["A) Normal Resting Eye Movement","B) Non-Reactive Eye Movement","C) Non-Rapid Eye Movement","D) Night Resting and Eye Mode"],correct_answer:"C) Non-Rapid Eye Movement",explanation:"NREM stands for Non-Rapid Eye Movement. During NREM sleep, the eyes do not move rapidly and the body is in a state of deep rest, during which tissue repair, muscle growth, and immune strengthening occur."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which type of sleep is most associated with dreaming and memory consolidation?",options:["A) NREM Stage 1","B) NREM Stage 2","C) NREM Stage 3 (deep sleep)","D) REM sleep"],correct_answer:"D) REM sleep",explanation:"REM (Rapid Eye Movement) sleep is the dreaming phase during which the brain is highly active. It is the stage most associated with memory consolidation \u2014 transferring new learning from short-term to long-term memory. The NREM stages are primarily for physical body repair."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How long does one complete sleep cycle (NREM + REM) last?",options:["A) 30 minutes","B) 60 minutes","C) 90 minutes","D) 120 minutes"],correct_answer:"C) 90 minutes",explanation:"One complete sleep cycle \u2014 moving through the NREM stages and then REM sleep \u2014 lasts approximately 90 minutes. A healthy night's sleep involves 4 to 5 such cycles, explaining why 7 to 8 hours is the recommended minimum for adults."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How many hours of sleep do students aged 10\u201312 need each night?",options:["A) 5 to 6 hours","B) 6 to 7 hours","C) 7 to 8 hours","D) 9 to 11 hours"],correct_answer:"D) 9 to 11 hours",explanation:"Children aged 10 to 12 need 9 to 11 hours of sleep per night. This is more than adults (7 to 8 hours) because their brains and bodies are growing rapidly, requiring more time for the repair and memory consolidation that happens during sleep."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is NOT an effect of sleep deprivation?",options:["A) Poor concentration in class","B) Improved memory and faster recall","C) Irritability and mood swings","D) Weakened immune system"],correct_answer:"B) Improved memory and faster recall",explanation:"Sleep deprivation does the opposite \u2014 it impairs memory and slows recall. The harmful effects of sleep deprivation include poor concentration, weakened immunity, mood swings, and memory loss. 'Improved memory' is incorrect; it is actually one of the first things to suffer when sleep is reduced."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the body's internal 24-hour clock that regulates sleep and wakefulness called?",options:["A) The glymphatic system","B) The melatonin cycle","C) The circadian rhythm","D) The NREM timer"],correct_answer:"C) The circadian rhythm",explanation:"The circadian rhythm is the body's natural 24-hour biological clock. It is regulated by light and darkness \u2014 daylight promotes alertness, while darkness triggers melatonin release and sleep. Screens before bed disrupt this rhythm by mimicking daylight with blue light."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which Bengaluru institution is known for research on sleep disorders and brain health?",options:["A) IISc (Indian Institute of Science)","B) NIMHANS (National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences)","C) ISRO (Indian Space Research Organisation)","D) RGICD (Rajiv Gandhi Institute of Chest Diseases)"],correct_answer:"B) NIMHANS (National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences)",explanation:"NIMHANS in Bengaluru, Karnataka, is India's premier institution for research on mental health, brain disorders, and sleep. Their studies have shown that sleep-deprived Indian school children perform significantly worse on memory and concentration tests."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the glymphatic system?",options:["A) The system that produces growth hormones during sleep","B) The brain's waste-clearance system that flushes toxins during sleep","C) The eye movement system that controls REM sleep","D) The body's immune defence system that works during NREM sleep"],correct_answer:"B) The brain's waste-clearance system that flushes toxins during sleep",explanation:"The glymphatic system is a recently discovered waste-clearance system in the brain. It is most active during deep sleep, flushing out harmful toxins that accumulate during waking hours. Without adequate sleep, these toxins build up and can damage long-term brain health."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why does using a phone or tablet before bed reduce sleep quality?",options:["A) The stories on phones are too exciting to allow relaxation","B) Screens emit blue light that suppresses melatonin and tricks the brain into thinking it is daytime","C) Screens produce sound waves that interfere with NREM sleep","D) The brightness of screens damages the eyes and causes pain that prevents sleep"],correct_answer:"B) Screens emit blue light that suppresses melatonin and tricks the brain into thinking it is daytime",explanation:"Screens emit blue light, which suppresses the production of melatonin \u2014 the hormone that signals the body to sleep. Without melatonin, the brain does not recognise that it is night-time, delaying sleep onset, reducing total sleep time, and cutting into valuable REM cycles."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student studies for 3 hours, then sleeps only 4 hours before an exam. Another student studies for 2 hours and sleeps 9 hours. Who is likely to perform better and why?",options:["A) The first student, because more study time means more information learned","B) The second student, because 9 hours of sleep allows full memory consolidation during REM","C) Both students will perform equally, since the total time spent (study + sleep) is the same","D) The first student, because the brain is most active just after studying"],correct_answer:"B) The second student, because 9 hours of sleep allows full memory consolidation during REM",explanation:"Memory consolidation happens during REM sleep. The second student's 9 hours ensures multiple complete sleep cycles including multiple REM phases, fully consolidating the 2 hours of study into long-term memory. The first student's 4 hours provides only 2\u20133 incomplete cycles, leaving much of the studied material poorly stored."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which hormone is released during deep NREM sleep and is especially important for children's growth?",options:["A) Insulin","B) Adrenaline","C) Growth hormone","D) Melatonin"],correct_answer:"C) Growth hormone",explanation:"During deep NREM sleep, the pituitary gland releases growth hormone, which repairs damaged tissues, builds muscle, and supports physical growth. This is why children need more sleep than adults \u2014 their bodies are actively growing and require more growth hormone production."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why is sleep described in the chapter as an 'active' process rather than a passive one?",options:["A) Because people move around a lot during sleep","B) Because the body and brain are performing essential repair, consolidation, and detox functions during sleep","C) Because sleep requires conscious effort to maintain","D) Because the heart beats faster during sleep than when awake"],correct_answer:"B) Because the body and brain are performing essential repair, consolidation, and detox functions during sleep",explanation:"Sleep is called active because the body and brain are far from idle. The brain consolidates memories (REM), the body releases growth hormones and repairs tissues (NREM), the immune system produces cytokines, and the glymphatic system clears brain toxins. These are complex, vital biological processes \u2014 not passive rest."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What are cytokines, and what role do they play in sleep?",options:["A) Brain chemicals that cause dreaming during REM sleep","B) Proteins produced by the immune system during sleep that help fight infection","C) Hormones that regulate the circadian rhythm","D) Toxins cleared by the glymphatic system during deep sleep"],correct_answer:"B) Proteins produced by the immune system during sleep that help fight infection",explanation:"Cytokines are proteins produced by the immune system, primarily during sleep. They help the body fight infection, inflammation, and stress. Sleep deprivation reduces cytokine production, weakening immunity and making the body more vulnerable to illness."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why cannot weekend 'catch-up' sleep fully compensate for sleep lost during the week?",options:["A) The body cannot sleep more than 8 hours even when trying to catch up","B) Cognitive damage to memory and concentration from weekday sleep loss cannot be fully reversed by weekend sleep","C) Weekend sleep is lighter and does not include REM phases","D) The circadian rhythm prevents the body from sleeping long on weekends"],correct_answer:"B) Cognitive damage to memory and concentration from weekday sleep loss cannot be fully reversed by weekend sleep",explanation:"Research shows that while weekend sleep provides some recovery, the specific cognitive damage from weekday sleep deprivation \u2014 particularly the failure to consolidate memories during REM \u2014 cannot be fully undone. Consistent daily sleep is far more effective than irregular patterns with weekend compensation."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In which stage of sleep does the body primarily repair muscles and release growth hormones?",options:["A) REM sleep","B) Light NREM (Stage 1)","C) Deep NREM (Stage 3)","D) The transition stage between NREM and REM"],correct_answer:"C) Deep NREM (Stage 3)",explanation:"The deepest stage of NREM sleep (Stage 3, also called slow-wave sleep) is when the body's physical restoration is most intensive. Growth hormones are released, muscles are repaired, and the immune system is strengthened. REM sleep is primarily for brain and memory functions, not physical repair."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following best explains why an athlete needs 8\u20139 hours of sleep?",options:["A) Athletes feel more tired than non-athletes and simply need more rest","B) Deep NREM sleep releases growth hormones that repair exercise-damaged muscles, while REM sleep consolidates motor-skill memory","C) Longer sleep gives athletes more time to dream about their sport, improving performance","D) Sleep reduces the lactic acid in muscles, which is why athletes need more of it"],correct_answer:"B) Deep NREM sleep releases growth hormones that repair exercise-damaged muscles, while REM sleep consolidates motor-skill memory",explanation:"Athletes benefit from sleep on two levels: NREM sleep releases growth hormones that repair the micro-tears caused by training, and REM sleep consolidates motor-skill memory \u2014 helping the brain 'practise' and encode physical skills learned during the day. Both together improve athletic performance and recovery."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is insomnia?",options:["A) An excess of REM sleep that causes vivid nightmares","B) A condition of sleeping too many hours per night","C) A sleep disorder characterised by persistent difficulty falling or staying asleep","D) A temporary tiredness caused by one night of poor sleep"],correct_answer:"C) A sleep disorder characterised by persistent difficulty falling or staying asleep",explanation:"Insomnia is a sleep disorder in which a person persistently struggles to fall asleep, stay asleep, or achieve restful sleep. It differs from temporary tiredness (one bad night). Insomnia is often caused by stress, anxiety, irregular sleep schedules, or excessive screen exposure before bed."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"If the glymphatic system only works during sleep, what long-term risk does chronic sleep deprivation pose to the brain?",options:["A) The brain grows too large due to toxin accumulation","B) Harmful toxins accumulate in the brain, increasing the risk of long-term neurological damage","C) The glymphatic system compensates by working harder during waking hours","D) Toxins are naturally expelled through other body systems, so there is no long-term risk"],correct_answer:"B) Harmful toxins accumulate in the brain, increasing the risk of long-term neurological damage",explanation:"The glymphatic system clears brain toxins only during sleep. Chronic sleep deprivation means these toxins \u2014 including proteins linked to Alzheimer's disease \u2014 are not cleared and accumulate over time. This is why long-term sleep deprivation is associated with increased risk of serious neurological conditions."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A student argues: 'Sleep is just rest \u2014 I can get the same benefit by lying quietly with my eyes closed.' Based on the chapter, why is this argument incorrect?",options:["A) The argument is correct \u2014 quiet rest provides the same benefits as sleep","B) It is incorrect because active biological processes like glymphatic cleansing, growth hormone release, and REM memory consolidation only occur during actual sleep, not quiet wakefulness","C) It is incorrect only because lying down is uncomfortable for long periods","D) It is incorrect because the body needs to be unconscious to conserve energy"],correct_answer:"B) It is incorrect because active biological processes like glymphatic cleansing, growth hormone release, and REM memory consolidation only occur during actual sleep, not quiet wakefulness",explanation:"The chapter clearly establishes that sleep is an active biological process with functions that cannot be replicated by quiet rest. Glymphatic toxin clearance, growth hormone release, immune cytokine production, and REM memory consolidation are all sleep-specific processes that require the brain to be in sleep states \u2014 not merely resting quietly while awake."}]}},2762,[]);